 #:seon.task{:name "a-conflicted-merge-request-has-no-repair-path"
             :plan "Rough live chain 1, merge repair (2026-10-14, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/live-chain-rough-1.md`): `(my.root/merge! {:seon.task/assignment \"51efd81cbc42\"})` answered `:seon.source/conflict-error` on `seon.ref/section` and `seon.ref/section-error` (main changed both since the merge base), with the message \"resolve on the branch and merge again\". That cannot work: `seon.program/three-way` keeps a declaration in conflict while the branch digest differs from both the base and the head, and no tool moves the source's merge base onto main's head or opens the merge branch AGENTS.md describes (forked from main, beside a fork of the agent's SCI context on the source branch, ours the target, theirs the source). Root's only outcome was `my.root/close!`, which deletes the source branch, and an ordinary commit of the repaired change by hand. Also: `my.root/merge-requests` offers `merge!` and `close!` but no read of the request's diff, so root read it in JVM mode through `seon.cluster.source/compare-branch` with a store taken from `seon.operator.runtime/running-instances`; and the docstring of `my.root/merge!` says nothing of a conflict. Done when a conflicted merge request offers the form that opens the merge branch holding both sides, root resolves there with ordinary `defn`, and `my.root/merge!` merges the resolution with its proofs; each entry of `my.root/merge-requests` offers its diff (`my.program/diff` from the source's side); and the conflict error's message names that form. Read `seon.merge/merge!`, `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` and `seon.program/three-way`; TASK-CHAIN S3b owns the final merge."}
